      "name": {
        "name": "Elongation factor 4",
        "short": "EF-4"
      },
      "description": [
        {
          "text": "<p>Elongation factor 4, also known as ribosomal back-translocase LepA, is required for accurate and efficient protein synthesis under certain stress conditions. Its function is not clear. However, it may act as a fidelity factor of the translation reaction, by catalysing a one-codon backward translocation of tRNAs on improperly translocated ribosomes. Back-translocation proceeds from a post-translocation (POST) complex to a pre-translocation (PRE) complex, thus giving elongation factor G a second chance to translocate the tRNAs correctly [[cite:PUB00056782], [cite:PUB00056783]]. Elongation factor 4 binds to ribosomes in a GTP-dependent manner. The eukaryotic homologue is known as GUF1 and promotes protein synthesis in chloroplasts and mitochondria [[cite:PUB00056784]].</p>",
          "llm": false,
          "checked": false,
          "updated": false
        }
      ],
